<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.9.2: Graphing Inequalities</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00518</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.9.2: Graphing Inequalities</md:title>
    <md:uuid>578511bb-4c17-498f-9ea5-720e2d22ff6f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <para>Work with a partner to match each of the following inequalities to its correct number line.</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_82b71fe3-ddfa-494d-adfd-b921d7eba72d" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 2. The number line to the right of 2 is highlighted.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_03_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>x&lt;2</para>
            <para>x≥2</para>
            <para>x&gt;2</para>
            <para>x≤2</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( x ≥ 2 \)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( x ≥ 2 \).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6a989b8b-f397-4c61-a70a-db4bb2a5eb49" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt=" A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.&#10;          ">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_03_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>x&lt;2</para>
            <para>x≥2</para>
            <para>x&gt;2</para>
            <para>x≤2</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( x &lt; 2 \)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( x &lt; 2 \).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_0a3fd630-41ba-4b06-930e-815bb5fd145e" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="A number line is shown. There is an open circle on 2. The number line to the right of 2 is highlighted.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_03_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>x&lt;2</para>
            <para>x≥2</para>
            <para>x&gt;2</para>
            <para>x≤2</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( x &gt; 2 \)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( x &gt; 2 \).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_08fc4001-c7f5-4f2c-a717-bb0201880022" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>
              <figure>
                <media alt="A number line is shown. There is a closed circle on 2. The number line to the left of 2 is highlighted.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_03_04.jpg"/>
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>x&lt;2</para>
            <para>x≥2</para>
            <para>x&gt;2</para>
            <para>x≤2</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \( x ≤ 2 \)</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \( x ≤ 2 \).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
      <note>
        <para>Why Should I Care?</para>
        <figure>
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_11_03_05.png"/>
          </media>
        </figure>
        <para>Nafy Flatley has worked to achieve her goal of bringing the food of Senegal to the world through her snack mix company, TERANGA. </para>
        <para>Coming up with snack mixes is not as simple as just combining ingredients. They have a lot of things to consider: What ingredients will they use? How much does each ingredient cost? What are the nutrititional values of each ingredient? How much does each ingredient weigh?</para>
        <para>If they can translate each question in a linear inequality, then they can use math to find mixes that meet their goals.</para>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
